{# Conference Navigator — the unified archive over the ESSC corpus (#756).
   One surface, two lenses: a by-person view (speakers-index.njk) and a
   by-paper view (papers-page.njk), both rendering from corpus.js /
   paperIndex.js. The wrapper page sets `archiveDefaultView` ("people" or
   "papers"); /speakers.html and /papers.html are redirects into this page
   with the matching view forced. archive.js toggles the panels and mirrors
   the choice in ?view=.

   Only the default view is server-rendered (#1641). The other panel is a
   placeholder that archive.js fills from anthology-fragment.njk on first
   activation. It used to ship in full behind `hidden`, which put both lists
   in one 1,530,626-byte document; the page is 891,549 bytes now. Either way
   only the default view reaches site search, since the panel that is not
   rendered cannot be indexed and the placeholder carries
   data-pagefind-ignore. #}
{% from "icons.njk" import icon %}
{%- set t = i18n[lang or "en"] -%}
{%- set n18n = t.navigator -%}
{%- set defaultView = archiveDefaultView or "people" -%}
{%- set coverageTip = n18n.coverageTip | replace("{a}", paperIndex.stats.abstractCount) | replace("{b}", paperIndex.stats.eligiblePaperCount) -%}

<header class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    {# No sub-brand mark above the eyebrow: the page header reads better without
       it, and the constellation still appears on this page in the Atlas signpost
       below (and on the Atlas page itself), which is where it does wayfinding
       work rather than decoration. #}
    <span class="eyebrow">{{ n18n.eyebrow }}</span>
    <h1>{{ n18n.title }}</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">{{ n18n.lead }}</p>
    <dl class="stats-row" aria-label="{{ n18n.eyebrow }}">
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>{{ corpus.stats.speakerCount }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ t.speakers.statSpeakers }}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>{{ corpus.stats.editions }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ t.speakers.statConferences }} <span class="muted">{{ t.speakers.statConferencesSince }}</span></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        {# Deduplicated paper count (paperIndex), so the headline matches the
           by-paper list and its filter dropdowns. corpus.stats.paperCount is
           the raw per-contribution total and double-counts the few papers that
           appear twice in the programmes (e.g. a poster shown on both days). #}
        <dt>{{ paperIndex.stats.paperCount }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ t.speakers.statPapers }}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>{{ paperIndex.stats.abstractCount }}<span class="coverage-tip" tabindex="0" role="img" data-tooltip="{{ coverageTip }}" title="{{ coverageTip }}" aria-label="{{ coverageTip }}">{{ icon("info") }}</span></dt>
        <dd>{{ n18n.statAbstracts }}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    {# The three deposit stickers, the same ones the repository README carries.
       Out in the open rather than inside the disclosure below: they are the
       fastest way to see that the corpus is deposited and archived, which is
       lost when they sit behind a summary nobody opens. Each carries its
       identifier in the alt text, so the DOI and the SWHID reach a screen
       reader without a text row repeating them.

       Served from our own assets rather than hotlinked. Zenodo throttles
       GitHub's image proxy, which is why the DOI badge intermittently renders
       broken on the README, and hotlinking would move that flakiness onto the
       site along with a third-party request per visitor. They are static SVGs,
       so refresh them when the DOI or the SWHID moves. The curl commands are
       in docs/corpus-archiving.md. #}
    <p class="deposit-badges">
      <a href="{{ site.corpus.doiUrl }}"><img src="/assets/images/badges/zenodo-doi.svg" alt="DOI {{ site.corpus.doi }}, deposited on Zenodo" width="191" height="20"></a>
      <a href="{{ site.corpus.swhOriginUrl }}"><img src="/assets/images/badges/swh-origin.svg" alt="Software repository archived in Software Heritage" width="140" height="20"></a>
      <a href="{{ site.corpus.swhidUrl }}"><img src="/assets/images/badges/swh-dir.svg" alt="Source tree archived in Software Heritage as {{ site.corpus.swhid }}" width="410" height="20"></a>
    </p>

    {# Wayfinding to the Anthology Atlas — the force-directed map of the same
       corpus the stats above count. In the header on purpose: at the foot of
       the page (where #1134 first put it) it sat ~87,000px down, below the
       whole 500-row list, so it was never seen.
       data-pagefind-ignore: it's a nav affordance, not content, and it links
       to a page that indexes itself. #}
    <div data-pagefind-ignore>
      {% include "atlas-signpost.njk" %}
    </div>

    {# Doors into the corpus by subject (#1644). The page opened on stats, two
       disclosures and then a wall of around 500 rows, and the only way to
       start from a subject was a <select> further down inside the filters. The
       seventeen theme pages built by #1255 were reachable from the Atlas and
       the sitemap and from nothing a reader of this page would find.

       They point at the theme's Atlas page rather than at
       ?view=papers&theme=…, which is the richer destination and links back to
       the filtered list itself. Locale suffix built here rather than carried
       on the data, since atlasThemePages is locale-agnostic and the theme
       pages are per locale (#1495).
       data-pagefind-ignore: a nav affordance pointing at pages that index
       themselves. #}
    <nav class="theme-doors" aria-labelledby="theme-doors-h" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <h2 class="theme-doors__h" id="theme-doors-h">{{ n18n.themeDoorsHeading }}</h2>
      <ul class="theme-doors__list">
        {%- set sfx = "" if (lang or "en") == "en" else "." + lang %}
        {%- for th in atlasThemePages %}
        <li><a href="/anthology-atlas/theme/{{ th.slug }}{{ sfx }}.html">{{ th.label[lang or "en"] or th.name }} <span class="theme-doors__n muted">{{ th.count }}</span></a></li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    {# Per-year abstract coverage, collapsed by default (native <details>, no
       JS). Bars render from paperIndex.years; coverage = abstracts on file /
       papers eligible for one. #}
    <details class="coverage-detail">
      <summary>{{ n18n.coverageSummary }}</summary>
      <div class="coverage-bars">
        {%- for y in paperIndex.years %}
        <div class="coverage-row">
          <span class="coverage-year">{{ y.year }}</span>
          <span class="coverage-track"><span class="coverage-fill" style="width: {{ y.coverage }}%"></span></span>
          <span class="coverage-count">{{ y.withAbstract }}<span class="coverage-of">/{{ y.eligible }}</span></span>
          <span class="coverage-pct">{{ y.coverage }}%</span>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p class="coverage-note">{{ n18n.coverageNote }}</p>
    </details>

    {# Renewal and collaboration by edition (#1566). Same disclosure treatment
       as the coverage bars above, and for the same reason: this header already
       carries the stats row, the badges and two panels, so a fourth block is
       closed by default. A real table rather than a bar list, because six
       columns of counts is what the question needs and a screen reader gets
       the header association for free. Rows from corpusRenewal.js. #}
    <details class="coverage-detail renewal-detail">
      <summary>{{ n18n.renewalSummary }}</summary>
      <div class="renewal-scroll">
        <table class="renewal-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColEdition }}</th>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColPapers }}</th>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColPeople }}</th>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColNew }}</th>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColSolo }}</th>
              <th scope="col">{{ n18n.renewalColPerPaper }}</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
          {%- for r in corpusRenewal.rows %}
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href="{{ r.url }}">{{ r.label }} {{ r.year }}</a></th>
              <td>{{ r.papers }}</td>
              <td>{{ r.authors }}</td>
              <td>{{ r.newcomerPct }}%</td>
              <td>{{ r.soloPct }}%</td>
              <td>{{ r.authorsPerPaper }}</td>
            </tr>
          {%- endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="coverage-note">{{ n18n.renewalNote }}</p>
    </details>

    {# "Cite this corpus" (#1221). The Anthology is deposited as a dataset on
       Zenodo, so it can be cited like any other source. Collapsed by default
       (native <details>, same treatment as the coverage bars) so it sits
       beside the stats without competing with them. In the header rather than
       the foot: #1134 learnt that hard, a footer block here lands ~87,000px
       down, below the whole list.
       The DOI is the concept DOI, resolving to the newest version — see the
       note on site.corpus in _data/site.js.
       ponytail: no copy-to-clipboard button. The reference sits in a <code>
       block the browser already lets you select and copy, and a button would
       be the only JS on this header. Add one if readers ask. #}
    <details class="cite-corpus">
      <summary>{{ n18n.citeSummary }}</summary>
      <p class="cite-corpus-note">{{ n18n.citeBody }}</p>
      <p class="cite-corpus-label">{{ n18n.citeLabel }}</p>
      <code class="cite-corpus-ref">{{ site.corpus.citation }}</code>
      {# The DOI and the SWHID are the badges above, so the panel keeps only
         what they cannot show: the corpus note in HAL, which describes what
         the dataset contains, how it is built and where it stops. #}
      <p class="cite-corpus-doi">
        <span class="cite-corpus-doi-label">{{ n18n.citeHalLabel }}</span>
        <a href="{{ site.corpus.halUrl }}">{{ site.corpus.halId }}</a>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">

    {# Module-level early-access notice. One element, every locale, via the
       navigator i18n catalog; the FR/DE bodies also flag that the individual
       paper pages are English-only. #}
    <aside class="anthology-notice" role="note">
      <span class="anthology-notice-badge">{{ n18n.earlyAccessBadge }}</span>
      {#- The figures come from releaseScope.js, which derives them from the
          abstract coverage the bars below already report, so the sentence and
          the bars cannot disagree (#1499). -#}
      {%- set _j = " " + (n18n.editionRangeJoiner or "to") + " " -%}
      {%- set _in = releaseScope.inScopeYears -%}
      {%- set _out = releaseScope.outOfScopeYears -%}
      {%- set inSpan = ("" ~ _in.first) if _in.first == _in.last else ("" ~ _in.first ~ _j ~ _in.last) -%}
      {%- set outSpan = ("" ~ _out.first) if _out.first == _out.last else ("" ~ _out.first ~ _j ~ _out.last) -%}
      <p class="anthology-notice-text">{{ n18n.earlyAccessBody
        | replace("{in}", inSpan)
        | replace("{out}", outSpan)
        | replace("{min}", releaseScope.minCoverage)
        | replace("{missing}", releaseScope.missingAbstracts)
        | replace("{eligible}", releaseScope.eligibleOut) }} <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}?subject=Anthology">{{ n18n.earlyAccessCta }}</a></p>
    </aside>

    {# data-msg-failed is the loader's error string (#1641), kept on the
       tablist rather than in the script so it stays localised the same way
       the filter status lines are. #}
    <div class="archive-toggle" role="tablist" aria-label="{{ n18n.title }}"
         data-msg-failed="{{ n18n.loadFailedView }}">
      <button type="button" role="tab" class="archive-tab" data-archive-tab="people"
              id="archive-tab-people" aria-controls="archive-panel-people"
              aria-selected="{{ 'true' if defaultView == 'people' else 'false' }}">{{ n18n.byPerson }}</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" class="archive-tab" data-archive-tab="papers"
              id="archive-tab-papers" aria-controls="archive-panel-papers"
              aria-selected="{{ 'true' if defaultView == 'papers' else 'false' }}">{{ n18n.byPaper }}</button>
    </div>

    {# Only the default view is server-rendered (#1641). The other panel used
       to ship in full behind `hidden` and `data-pagefind-ignore`, which is
       639,520 of this page's 1,530,626 bytes doing nothing for the reader who
       never opens it. It arrives from /fragments/anthology-<view>.<lang>.html
       the first time its tab is activated, and stays for the rest of the
       visit, so the toggle is instant from the second press on.

       The placeholder holds the loading line, which the loader replaces with
       the panel or with an error the reader can retry by pressing the tab
       again. Scripting was already required to reach the second view at all,
       since the tablist is wired in archive.js and both panels are otherwise
       `hidden`, so nothing that worked without it stops working. #}
    <div class="archive-panel" id="archive-panel-people" role="tabpanel"
         aria-labelledby="archive-tab-people" data-archive-panel="people"
         {%- if defaultView != 'people' %} hidden data-pagefind-ignore data-archive-src="/fragments/anthology-people.{{ lang or 'en' }}.html"{% endif %}>
      {%- if defaultView == 'people' %}
      {% include "speakers-index.njk" %}
      {%- else %}
      <p class="archive-panel__status" data-archive-status>{{ n18n.loadingView }}</p>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="archive-panel" id="archive-panel-papers" role="tabpanel"
         aria-labelledby="archive-tab-papers" data-archive-panel="papers"
         {%- if defaultView != 'papers' %} hidden data-pagefind-ignore data-archive-src="/fragments/anthology-papers.{{ lang or 'en' }}.html"{% endif %}>
      {%- if defaultView == 'papers' %}
      {% include "papers-page.njk" %}
      {%- else %}
      <p class="archive-panel__status" data-archive-status>{{ n18n.loadingView }}</p>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

{# Design credit (#1228). The site-wide footer credit is at the level nobody
   reads it at, and someone citing or writing about the Anthology has nothing
   to attribute the design to. Same name, slug and register as
   footer.authorship, so the two read as one claim rather than competing ones.
   data-pagefind-ignore: a credit line is not page content to search. #}
<p class="page-credit" data-pagefind-ignore>
  {{ t.footer.credit.anthology }}
  <a href="{{ '/board.html' | localizedHref(lang) }}#{{ t.footer.credit.authorSlug }}">{{ t.footer.credit.authorName }}</a>
</p>

<script src="{{ '/assets/js/archive.js' | bust }}" defer></script>
